<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Coordinator.Atlas.Core — the Core half of desktop spatial truth.

    CORE project: `net9.0`, no Windows suffix, zero Windows dependencies. It holds the model of what
    the desktop is — monitors, work areas, scaling, windows and their geometry — and the pure layout
    math over it. It does not enumerate anything. Monitor and window enumeration, DPI queries, the
    visible-bounds query and the position call belong to a Windows-targeted adapter project that
    does not exist yet (docs/ATLAS.md §8 calls it Coordinator.Atlas.Shell).

    This project is the sharpest demonstration of why the split exists (ADR 0003). The zone-rectangle
    math in LayoutMath.cs is the hardest part of the first module to get right and the cheapest part
    to verify: it is arithmetic over values, so it runs and is unit-tested on Linux, in a container,
    with no monitor attached.

    Note there is deliberately NO ProjectReference here. Atlas needs nothing from the platform:
    geometry does not have capabilities, and adding a reference "for consistency" would be a
    dependency with no consumer.

    COMPILES. Built by GitHub Actions on Ubuntu and Windows at 7aef6ff (2026-08-08), 0 warnings,
    and covered by Coordinator.Atlas.Core.Tests (25 tests, all passing).

    Shared properties come from Directory.Build.props at the repository root.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Coordinator.Atlas</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Coordinator.Atlas.Core</AssemblyName>
    <Description>Windows Coordinator — Atlas (Core): the coherent desktop snapshot, coordinate-space-tagged geometry, and the pure zone-rectangle math. Contains no Windows types.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
